=========================================================
SOLID PRINCIPLES – INTERVIEW NOTES (COPYABLE)
=========================================================

SOLID is a set of five object-oriented design principles that help build software that is:

• Easy to maintain
• Easy to extend
• Loosely coupled
• Highly cohesive
• Testable
• Scalable

=========================================================
S → SINGLE RESPONSIBILITY PRINCIPLE (SRP)
=========================================================

Definition
----------
"A class should have only ONE reason to change."

In simple words,

One class = One responsibility = One job.

Good Example
------------

class Invoice
{
public:
    void calculateTotal();
};

class InvoicePrinter
{
public:
    void print();
};

class InvoiceRepository
{
public:
    void saveToDatabase();
};

Each class has a single responsibility.

Bad Example
-----------

class Invoice
{
public:
    void calculateTotal();
    void printInvoice();
    void saveToDatabase();
};

This class has multiple responsibilities.

Problems
--------
• Difficult to maintain
• Difficult to test
• One change may affect unrelated functionality

Real-world Example
------------------

Employee

Instead of

Employee
    • Calculate Salary
    • Print Salary
    • Save Salary
    • Send Email

Split into

Employee
SalaryCalculator
SalaryPrinter
EmailService

Interview Answer
----------------

"SRP states that a class should have only one responsibility and therefore only one reason to change."



=========================================================
O → OPEN CLOSED PRINCIPLE (OCP)
=========================================================

Definition
----------

"Software entities should be OPEN for extension but CLOSED for modification."

Meaning

You should add new functionality without changing existing code.

Bad Example
-----------

class Payment
{
public:

    void pay(string type)
    {
        if(type=="CreditCard")
        {
        }
        else if(type=="UPI")
        {
        }
        else if(type=="PayPal")
        {
        }
    }
};

Every new payment method requires modifying this class.

Good Example
------------

class Payment
{
public:
    virtual void pay() = 0;
};

class CreditCard : public Payment
{
};

class UPI : public Payment
{
};

class PayPal : public Payment
{
};

Adding ApplePay requires creating a new class.

Existing code remains unchanged.

Real-world Example
------------------

Notification System

Instead of modifying Notification every time,

Create

EmailNotification
SMSNotification
PushNotification

Interview Answer
----------------

"OCP means software should allow adding new functionality without modifying existing, tested code."



=========================================================
L → LISKOV SUBSTITUTION PRINCIPLE (LSP)
=========================================================

Definition
----------

"Derived classes should be replaceable with their base class without changing program correctness."

Meaning

Anywhere a base class is expected,

Derived class should work correctly.

Bad Example
-----------

class Bird
{
public:
    virtual void fly();
};

class Penguin : public Bird
{
public:
    void fly()
    {
        throw exception();
    }
};

Penguin cannot fly.

Inheritance is wrong.

Good Design
-----------

class Bird
{
};

class FlyingBird : public Bird
{
public:
    virtual void fly();
};

class Sparrow : public FlyingBird
{
};

class Penguin : public Bird
{
};

Now LSP is satisfied.

Real-world Example
------------------

Rectangle and Square

Square should not inherit Rectangle if changing width and height independently breaks expected behavior.

Interview Answer
----------------

"LSP states that derived classes should be usable wherever the base class is expected without breaking the correctness of the program."



=========================================================
I → INTERFACE SEGREGATION PRINCIPLE (ISP)
=========================================================

Definition
----------

"Clients should not be forced to depend on methods they do not use."

Meaning

Prefer many small interfaces over one large interface.

Bad Example
-----------

class Machine
{
public:

    virtual void print() = 0;
    virtual void scan() = 0;
    virtual void fax() = 0;
};

SimplePrinter must implement scan() and fax() unnecessarily.

Good Example
------------

class Printer
{
public:
    virtual void print() = 0;
};

class Scanner
{
public:
    virtual void scan() = 0;
};

class Fax
{
public:
    virtual void fax() = 0;
};

SimplePrinter implements only Printer.

Real-world Example
------------------

Restaurant

Vegetarian customer should not be forced to order non-vegetarian items.

Separate menus.

Interview Answer
----------------

"ISP recommends creating small, focused interfaces so clients implement only the functionality they actually need."



=========================================================
D → DEPENDENCY INVERSION PRINCIPLE (DIP)
=========================================================

Definition
----------

"High-level modules should not depend on low-level modules.
Both should depend on abstractions."

Meaning

Depend on interfaces,

Not concrete classes.

Bad Example
-----------

class Keyboard
{
};

class Computer
{
private:
    Keyboard keyboard;
};

Computer is tightly coupled to Keyboard.

Good Example
------------

class InputDevice
{
public:
    virtual void input() = 0;
};

class Keyboard : public InputDevice
{
};

class Mouse : public InputDevice
{
};

class Computer
{
private:
    InputDevice* device;
};

Computer works with Keyboard, Mouse, TouchScreen, etc.

Real-world Example
------------------

Car

Car depends on Engine interface.

PetrolEngine
DieselEngine
ElectricEngine

Any engine can be plugged in.

Interview Answer
----------------

"DIP states that high-level modules should depend on abstractions rather than concrete implementations, reducing coupling and improving flexibility."



=========================================================
SOLID SUMMARY TABLE
=========================================================

S → Single Responsibility Principle
-----------------------------------
One class should have only one responsibility.

Keyword:
One Job

-----------------------------------

O → Open Closed Principle
-------------------------
Open for extension.
Closed for modification.

Keyword:
Extend, Don't Modify

-----------------------------------

L → Liskov Substitution Principle
---------------------------------
Derived class should completely replace base class.

Keyword:
Substitutability

-----------------------------------

I → Interface Segregation Principle
-----------------------------------
Create small interfaces.

Keyword:
Don't force unnecessary methods

-----------------------------------

D → Dependency Inversion Principle
----------------------------------
Depend on abstractions, not implementations.

Keyword:
Loose Coupling



=========================================================
MOST ASKED INTERVIEW QUESTIONS
=========================================================

Q1. What is SOLID?

A collection of five object-oriented design principles that improve maintainability, extensibility, flexibility, and testability.

---------------------------------------------------------

Q2. Which SOLID principle is violated if one class prints, saves, and emails invoices?

Answer:
Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)

---------------------------------------------------------

Q3. Which principle says "Open for extension but closed for modification"?

Answer:
Open Closed Principle (OCP)

---------------------------------------------------------

Q4. Which SOLID principle promotes loose coupling?

Answer:
Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)

---------------------------------------------------------

Q5. Which principle recommends many small interfaces?

Answer:
Interface Segregation Principle (ISP)

---------------------------------------------------------

Q6. Penguin cannot fly. Which SOLID principle is violated?

Answer:
Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP)

---------------------------------------------------------

Q7. Why are interfaces used in DIP?

Answer:
Interfaces reduce coupling, improve flexibility, simplify testing, and allow implementations to be swapped without changing high-level code.

